**Why disk scheduling.** On a hard disk the head must move (seek) to the track, which costs milliseconds. Many processes issue requests, so the OS reorders them to **minimise total head movement**, increase throughput and keep waiting time fair.

**Algorithms.**
- **FCFS:** serve in arrival order. Fair but lots of head movement.
- **SSTF:** nearest request first. Good throughput but can **starve** far requests.
- **SCAN (elevator):** sweep in one direction to the end, then reverse. **C-SCAN:** serve in one direction only, jump back, for more uniform waits.
- **LOOK / C-LOOK:** like SCAN/C-SCAN but turn around at the last request, not the disk end.
There is no universally "best". LOOK/C-LOOK are typically preferred for balancing throughput and fairness on loaded rotational disks; SSDs have no seek, so scheduling matters much less.

**Implementing LOOK (moving up first), O(n log n) for sorting.**

```
int look(vector<int> req, int head) {
    sort(req.begin(), req.end());
    int total = 0, pos = head;
    for (int r : req) if (r >= head) { total += r - pos; pos = r; }
    for (int i = (int)req.size() - 1; i >= 0; --i)
        if (req[i] < head) { total += pos - req[i]; pos = req[i]; }
    return total;
}
```

**Memory types.** **Registers** (fastest, tiny), **cache** (L1/L2/L3, SRAM), **main memory** (DRAM), **secondary storage** (SSD/HDD), each larger, slower and cheaper per byte.

**Caches.** They exploit **temporal and spatial locality**: recently or nearby accessed data is likely to be used again. Concepts: hit and miss, cache lines, **mapping** (direct-mapped, set-associative, fully associative), replacement (LRU, FIFO), write policy (write-through, write-back). Types: CPU caches (L1 instruction/data, L2, L3), TLB, disk and browser caches.

**LRU cache.** O(1) get and put need two structures: a **hash map** from key to node, and a **doubly linked list** ordered by recency. On access, move the node to the front; on insert beyond capacity, evict the node at the tail and erase it from the map. In C++, `unordered_map<int, list<pair<int,int>>::iterator>` plus `std::list`.
